REGENT OF BADUNG

REGIONAL REGULATION OF BADUNG REGENCY

NUMBER 15 OF 2013

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF BADUNG,

Considering:
a. that every citizen has the right to obtain a sense of security and freedom from all forms of Violence;
b. that Women and Children are among vulnerable groups who tend to experience Violence which constitutes an act that violates human rights and need to receive legal Protection;
c. that the implementation of Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence in Badung Regency needs to be supported by institutions and regulations so as to guarantee its implementation;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;

In View of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 69 of 1958 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions within the Level I Regions of Bali, West Nusa Tenggara and East Nusa Tenggara (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1958 Number 122, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1655);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
8. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
9. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
10. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning the Division of Government Affairs Between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/Municipal Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
11. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Women's Protection;
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Handling Child Victims of Violence;
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence;
18. Regional Regulation of Badung Regency Number 4 of 2008 concerning Government Affairs that are the Authority of Badung Regency (Regional Gazette of Badung Regency of 2008 Number 4, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Badung Regency Number 4);

With Mutual Approval

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of Women and Child Victims of Violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of Victims' rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of Victims; and
e. legal certainty.

CHAPTER III

VICTIMS' RIGHTS

Article 4

Every Woman and Child Victim of Violence has the right:
a. to have their dignity respected as a human being;
b. to receive Protection from family, community, Regional Government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on a court protection order;
c. to restoration of physical, psychological and sexual health according to the suffering experienced by Victims of Violence;
d. to special handling related to the confidentiality of Victims;
e. to assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. to spiritual guidance services; and
g. to determine their own decisions.

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 5

1. The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility to provide Protection to Women and Child Victims of Violence.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. implementing policies for Protection of Women and Child Victims of Violence based on laws and regulations;
b. establishing policies, programs and activities for Protection of Women and Child Victims of Violence;
c. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of Protection of Women and Child Victims of Violence;
d. allocating budget in the implementation of Protection of Women and Child Victims of Violence;
e. fostering and supervising the implementation of Protection of Women and Child Victims of Violence.

Article 6

1. The community has the obligation and responsibility to provide Protection to Women and Child Victims of Violence.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out in the form of:
a. preventing the occurrence of acts of Violence against Women and Children;
b. providing Protection to Victims;
c. providing emergency assistance;
d. providing information and/or reporting acts of Violence against women and Children to the competent authorities; and
e. participating in the handling of Victims of Violence.

CHAPTER V

INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

Article 7

1. In implementing Protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence in the Region, the Regent may establish an IPU.
2. The establishment and development of IPU shall be adjusted to the development of priority needs, and the financial capacity of the Region as well as the institutional and personnel capacity available in the Region.
3. The IPU as referred to in paragraph (1) may accommodate institutions/work units that provide services for Victims of Violence such as P2TP2A, Safe Houses, Child Social Protection Houses, Trauma Center Protection Houses, Women's Social Protection Houses, Shelters and other service activities in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
4. Forms of services implemented by the IPU include:  a. complaints;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. legal aid;
e. repatriation; andf. social reintegration.
5. In providing Protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence, the IPU is obligated to:a. provide services as quickly as possible and free of charge to Victims;
b. implement Protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to Health Rehabilitation, Social Rehabilitation, Repatriation, social reintegration and legal aid;
c. cooperate with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants required for Victims;
d. network with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of Victims and cooperate with witness and Victim protection institutions, social protection houses or trauma centers owned by the government, community, or other institutions for the health recovery of Victims;
e. provide convenience, comfort, and safety for Victims;
f. maintain confidentiality of Victims;
g. provide fulfillment of legal aid for Victims;
h. in the event that integrated service delivery is carried out in a networked manner, the IPU remains responsible for the entire referral process of services required by Victims.
6. The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be implemented in accordance with MSS.

CHAPTER VI

MINIMUM SERVICE STANDARDS

Article 9

MSS for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence include services for:
a. handling complaints/reports of Victims of Violence against Women and Children;
b. health services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
c. social rehabilitation for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
d. law enforcement and legal aid for Women and Children Victims of Violence; ande. repatriation and social reintegration for Women and Children Victims of Violence.

Article 10

The MSS as referred to in Article 9 has performance indicators including:
a. coverage of Women and Children Victims of Violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers in integrated service units;
b. coverage of Women and Children Victims of Violence who receive health services by trained health personnel at Community Health Centers and Hospitals;
c. coverage of Social Rehabilitation services provided by trained Social Rehabilitation officers for Women and Children Victims of Violence in integrated service units;
d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for Women and Children Victims of Violence in integrated service units;
e. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level to court decisions on cases of Violence against Women and Children;
f. coverage of Women and Children Victims of Violence who receive legal aid services;
g. coverage of Repatriation services for Women and Children Victims of Violence; andh. coverage of social reintegration services for Women and Children Victims of Violence.
